WEBVTT

00:00:00.544 --> 00:00:19.528
<v Casey>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got five Nuu-chah-nulth nations building their own salmon genomics lab so they stop depending on one DFO facility for every stock decision on the coast — plus a federal audit showing most DFO science barely touches Indigenous knowledge at all. Who gets to count as an authority? That and more, coming right up.

00:00:33.003 --> 00:00:56.915
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Thursday, September 24th, 2026, and it's the National Day for Truth and Reconciliation. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Indigenous Lands &amp; Innovation.

00:00:57.694 --> 00:01:07.798
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Today we're looking at how nations here and up the coast are building their own tools instead of waiting for someone else's system to make room for them.

00:01:07.898 --> 00:01:39.418
<v Riley>Quick weather note before we get going. Overcast at Horsefly Lake this morning, around six degrees, with a high of fifteen and a low of five. Expect dense drizzle through the day — close to a hundred percent chance of precipitation, maybe four millimetres by evening. Around the region, 100 Mile House is sitting near four with partly cloudy skies, Williams Lake is at five and overcast, Quesnel is at four and overcast, and Alexis Creek is holding at three, also overcast. Grey morning across the whole district.

00:01:39.862 --> 00:01:43.374
<v Casey>Grab a coat. Let's get into what's happened around here since yesterday.

00:01:44.774 --> 00:02:17.374
<v Casey>A good chunk of today's news is close to home, and a fair amount of it comes back to fire, land, and who's steering the response. BC Wildfire Service has a prescribed burn planned near Thunder Mountain Speedway in Williams Lake. It's running alongside Williams Lake First Nation, Alkali Resource Management, and the Cariboo Chilcotin Natural Resource District. The idea behind a prescribed burn is simple — you take fuel off the ground on a day you chose, with crews standing by and weather cooperating, instead of waiting for a wildfire to do it in August, with a subdivision downwind.

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:20.492
<v Riley>Cheaper to pick the day than have it picked for you.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:38.788
<v Riley>Downtown, a different kind of fire. Firefighters responded to a vehicle fire in Williams Lake Wednesday morning. Photos from the scene show the car burned down close to the frame by the time crews got it out. No cause has been released yet, and nobody was reported hurt.

00:02:40.119 --> 00:03:04.671
<v Casey>Quesnel City Council is putting something in writing. They voted to send a letter to the Premier asking for the city to be included as an active partner in a proposed Forestry Crisis Action Group, and for recognition as a provincial hub for forestry innovation. Council members disagreed over how specific the letter should get — one councillor pushed to keep it general, while the mayor wanted a direct mention of idle land resources.

00:03:05.951 --> 00:03:13.759
<v Riley>Worth watching whether Williams Lake or 100 Mile House sign onto something similar, or if Quesnel's carrying this one alone.

00:03:15.197 --> 00:03:27.637
<v Riley>Former BC Green leader Andrew Weaver announced he's running again, this time as a CentreBC candidate. He's contesting Oak Bay-Gordon Head, the same Victoria-area riding he represented for years as an MLA.

00:03:28.804 --> 00:03:49.940
<v Casey>In West Quesnel, there was a ceremony this week at the future site of a new long-term care facility. The land has been blessed ahead of construction. The project comes from Providence Living, planned as a two-hundred-eighty-eight-bed facility on Roddis Drive, in what's being called Cariboo Field — a net gain of over two hundred beds once the older Dunrovin Park Lodge closes.

00:03:51.156 --> 00:04:04.764
<v Riley>That's a large facility for this region, one of the bigger care projects the Cariboo has seen in years. A land blessing before groundbreaking says the project is being brought into the community rather than dropped onto it.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:14.871
<v Casey>The second annual Bulls &amp; Baby Buckers rodeo drew a strong crowd in Williams Lake this week, and by most accounts it's already a fan favourite in only its second year.

00:04:16.142 --> 00:04:30.086
<v Riley>And down in the Okanagan, south and east of us — different region, worth being clear about that — boaters pulled a bear cub out of the middle of Okanagan Lake. One witness said she'd never seen anything like it in years on the water.

00:04:30.606 --> 00:04:32.846
<v Casey>Lucky bear, and lucky boaters who noticed.

00:04:34.375 --> 00:04:51.767
<v Casey>Job losses keep turning up in unexpected corners of the tech and entertainment industries. Kurt Kuhlmann, a twenty-year veteran of Bethesda and the studio's Elder Scrolls lore keeper, is still job hunting months after being laid off. He told reporters he's open to any role that's a reasonable match for his skills.

00:04:53.144 --> 00:04:57.448
<v Riley>Twenty years of institutional memory, and the market apparently doesn't have a line item for it.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:28.608
<v Riley>On the water side, a coalition of Mexican and international scientists is condemning plans to industrialize the Gulf of California with LNG terminals. They're calling the Gulf the Aquarium of the World for a reason — whales, porpoises, manta rays, and a dense reef fish population sit in the exact stretch of water proposed for terminal construction. One of three planned LNG projects there has already been stopped on financial and legal grounds. This fight is over the remaining two.

00:05:29.159 --> 00:05:31.047
<v Casey>So the industry's zero for one so far.

00:05:32.487 --> 00:05:50.231
<v Casey>From Lassen Volcanic National Park in California, researchers have identified a new species of amoeba, Incendiamoeba cascadensis, that can eat, move, and reproduce in water as hot as sixty-three degrees Celsius. That's a hundred forty-five degrees Fahrenheit, and it's a record for any complex cell, not just amoebas.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:53.630
<v Riley>Life finding a foothold nobody thought was survivable.

00:05:55.091 --> 00:06:17.523
<v Riley>A few stories from further afield. In Louisiana, a Republican-controlled legislature's redistricting plan has cost Representative Cleo Fields his seat for the second time, tied directly to Supreme Court rulings that weakened protections against racial gerrymandering. Fields is one of only four Black representatives Louisiana has sent to Congress since Reconstruction.

00:06:19.495 --> 00:06:35.823
<v Casey>On the East Coast, a technical failure grounded flights at several major airports on Monday. Operations resumed by evening, but with lingering delays, and it's reviving old questions about how much longer the FAA's air traffic control infrastructure can be patched together before it needs a real rebuild.

00:06:37.107 --> 00:06:56.763
<v Riley>Back closer to home, the Vancouver Sun is reporting that BC's snap provincial election call, coming just three days into the municipal campaign period, has thrown local political organizers for a loop. Some are now weighing whether a single candidate could legally run in both races at the same time, which nobody seems to have planned for.

00:06:57.878 --> 00:07:04.230
<v Casey>That connects to the Weaver story — the timing question runs through every riding this fall, not just Oak Bay-Gordon Head.

00:07:05.671 --> 00:07:21.447
<v Casey>And Hackaday has a piece on how Pixar, a studio once devoted entirely to Silicon Graphics workstations, ended up rebuilding its production pipeline on Linux over the years. Even the biggest, best-funded studios in the world eventually get pushed off proprietary hardware they once swore by.

00:07:22.861 --> 00:07:44.221
<v Riley>Last story, and it's a fitting one to close on. Engadget tested whether a wired gaming mouse still beats a wireless one in real performance terms — a debate that used to carry real weight for competitive players. Their finding after direct testing: there's barely a difference left in latency, and battery life on the wireless side has closed most of the old gap.

00:07:44.843 --> 00:07:47.875
<v Casey>A whole category of anxiety, solved by better batteries.

00:07:48.981 --> 00:07:59.861
<v Riley>For years the advice was always "get the wired one if it matters." That's not true anymore. Decent note to end on before we move into a story about a different kind of catching up.

00:08:00.382 --> 00:08:02.518
<v Casey>On that, let's get into today's Spotlight.

00:08:03.918 --> 00:08:32.438
<v Riley>Today's spotlight is the Cariboo Friendship Society, which has been serving Williams Lake since 1969. They run housing programs, youth services, crisis support, and The Hearth restaurant, and on the National Day for Truth and Reconciliation, their work sits close to the actual point of the date. This is culturally grounded support that's shown up for this community for fifty-seven years, not just on the one day a year set aside to think about it.

00:08:33.082 --> 00:08:41.410
<v Casey>Fifty-seven years of showing up is its own kind of record, and it rarely gets written down anywhere official. You can find them at cariboofriendshipsociety.ca.

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:44.790
<v Riley>Which is a decent way into what we're digging into today.

00:08:46.190 --> 00:09:04.382
<v Riley>A bigger question sits underneath a Tyee story worth pulling out directly. If the genetic data that tells you which salmon stock is which lives in one federal lab, do those fish exist as a known, managed resource for the nations whose rivers they return to? Or only for whoever holds the keys to that building?

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:06.932
<v Casey>That's the sharper version of it. Keep going.

00:09:07.456 --> 00:09:42.016
<v Riley>Five Nuu-chah-nulth nations are building their own genomics capacity for salmon stock identification so they stop depending entirely on the Department of Fisheries and Oceans to run that analysis. Candace Picco at Ha'oom Fisheries Society and Dr. Ben Sutherland out of Vancouver Island University are leading the development of open-source tools to decentralize the work. Coastwide genetic stock identification for salmon across all of western Canada currently runs through one facility — the Molecular Genetics Laboratory at DFO's Pacific Biological Station.

00:09:42.306 --> 00:09:43.778
<v Casey>One lab. For the whole coast.

00:09:44.372 --> 00:10:04.404
<v Riley>One lab, for every river, for every nation, for every conservation and allocation decision that depends on knowing which stock a given fish belongs to. That's a structural bottleneck sitting underneath decades of fisheries management, and it answers to Ottawa, not to the people whose rivers those fish are actually returning to.

00:10:04.989 --> 00:10:09.877
<v Casey>So the sovereignty language in that piece isn't just branding for a grant application.

00:10:10.518 --> 00:10:26.246
<v Riley>No. Any organization that discovered its most critical function ran through one facility with no backup would treat that as an emergency and fix it fast. DFO's critical function here is stock identification, and until this project, it had one node, full stop.

00:10:26.784 --> 00:10:52.600
<v Casey>The history behind that framing is worse than a supply-chain risk. There's a peer-reviewed paper, "Taking Care of Knowledge, Taking Care of Salmon," that documents DFO and other institutions collecting Indigenous knowledge about salmon without consent, historically, to build up Canada's commercial fishing sector. When this project talks about sovereignty over the data, there's an actual paper trail behind why that word gets used instead of something softer.

00:10:53.131 --> 00:11:03.123
<v Riley>Which is what makes decentralizing the lab more than a technical upgrade. It's correcting a specific, documented history, not just adding redundancy for its own sake.

00:11:03.662 --> 00:11:30.654
<v Casey>Where I get stuck is what happens after the grant runs out. This is funded for three years — the First Nations Fisheries Council's Indigenous Stewardship Fund, the Pacific Salmon Foundation, and Genome British Columbia. Building the tool is the achievable part. The harder question is whether DFO treats nation-run genetic data as equal evidence the next time there's an allocation dispute, or files it as a supplementary appendix nobody reads before the decision's already made.

00:11:30.981 --> 00:11:35.037
<v Riley>That's the real test, and it's a governance test, not a technical one.

00:11:35.583 --> 00:11:53.135
<v Casey>There's a federal audit from recent months, covered by Canada's National Observer, that found roughly eighty-seven percent of DFO's own scientific documents don't draw on Indigenous knowledge in any substantive way — despite the department's stated commitment to partnership in fish recovery work being on the record for years.

00:11:53.315 --> 00:11:54.291
<v Riley>Eighty-seven percent.

00:11:54.866 --> 00:12:01.722
<v Casey>That's not a rounding error. That's the baseline this genomics project has to work against, no matter how good the science is.

00:12:02.381 --> 00:12:23.453
<v Riley>Which is why building your own instrument matters more here than it would somewhere with a better track record. If the department won't reliably weigh your knowledge when you hand it over voluntarily, the next move is producing independently verifiable science under your own name, generated with your own equipment, that's harder to wave off as anecdotal.

00:12:23.982 --> 00:12:46.454
<v Casey>Unless it gets waved off anyway. There's a comparable pattern out east. After the Marshall decision in 1999 affirmed treaty-protected Indigenous fishing rights in Atlantic Canada, DFO still didn't meaningfully adjust its management practices to account for Indigenous livelihood fishers. A Supreme Court ruling, and decades later the department's approach on the ground had barely moved.

00:12:47.080 --> 00:13:00.200
<v Riley>So a court win didn't force the structural change on its own. A working tool might do something a ruling couldn't, because it doesn't need DFO's permission to exist in the first place. Nobody has to approve a lab.

00:13:00.849 --> 00:13:16.865
<v Casey>Or the tool gets treated the same way the court ruling did. Owning the equipment isn't automatically the same as having your numbers recognized. Recognition is a separate, ongoing fight, and it's the one that actually decides whether any of this changes an allocation outcome five years from now.

00:13:17.505 --> 00:13:43.425
<v Riley>Granted. But there's a real difference between walking into that fight with nothing and walking in with your own defensible numbers. Right now, if a nation disputes a DFO stock assessment, they're disputing it using DFO's own data, generated in DFO's own lab, interpreted by DFO's own scientists. There's no independent check possible in that scenario. This project changes that math before it changes a single decision.

00:13:44.039 --> 00:14:03.399
<v Casey>That's a real shift. Evidentiary standing didn't exist before this, and it might now. What's still missing is anything confirming that standing gets weighed the same as DFO's own numbers in a permitting or allocation call made under deadline pressure. Owning the equipment isn't the same as having a formal seat at the table when the decision gets made.

00:14:03.983 --> 00:14:15.327
<v Riley>No argument there. That's a governance fight, separate from the science, and the science was the part these five nations could actually control this year with the money and time they had.

00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:39.946
<v Casey>Which is worth separating from the funding question, because that's usually where stories like this land and it's the wrong place to end up. The interesting piece isn't whether the three-year grant renews. It's whether the open-source tools get built so a lab technician trained through Ha'oom Fisheries Society can run and maintain the whole pipeline without calling Vancouver Island University every time something breaks.

00:14:40.485 --> 00:14:54.477
<v Riley>That's the sovereignty test. Not who paid for the sequencer — who can operate it in year four without an outside call, and who trains the second person so the whole capacity doesn't collapse the day one biologist takes a better-paying job elsewhere.

00:14:55.001 --> 00:15:06.185
<v Casey>Because that's the failure mode with these projects historically. One skilled person becomes the entire system, and the system stops quietly when they leave. Sorry — stops when they leave.

00:15:06.717 --> 00:15:33.581
<v Riley>A version of that same test is already running here in the Cariboo, and it didn't need a genomics lab to prove the model. Williams Lake First Nation and Alkali Resource Management are co-managing this week's prescribed burn near Thunder Mountain Speedway with BC Wildfire Service, not as consulted stakeholders getting briefed after the plan's finished, but as operational partners deciding where and when the fire actually goes on the ground.

00:15:34.139 --> 00:15:39.859
<v Casey>That's fire, not fish. Different agency, different history of who controlled the data in the first place.

00:15:40.441 --> 00:16:00.481
<v Riley>Different field, same underlying shift — a nation holding operational authority over a resource decision, instead of receiving a memo about one that's already been made somewhere else. The genomics project is trying to move fisheries management toward the footing fire management has already reached, at least in this valley.

00:16:01.094 --> 00:16:14.574
<v Casey>Alkali Resource Management spent years building that fire relationship before it started reading as routine instead of exceptional. Fisheries genomics is starting from a lab that's had a total, uncontested monopoly for decades. Steeper hill.

00:16:15.251 --> 00:16:22.259
<v Riley>Steeper hill, sure. Doesn't mean the direction's wrong. Fire had a head start on trust that fisheries hasn't earned yet.

00:16:22.823 --> 00:16:30.511
<v Casey>Agreed on direction. Where we land differently is how much a working tool, on its own, changes who has to say yes at the end of the process.

00:16:31.187 --> 00:16:49.883
<v Riley>Something concrete worth watching, and it isn't a funding number: whether Ha'oom Fisheries Society's stock data gets cited as primary evidence, not supporting evidence, in a single DFO allocation decision inside that three-year window. That's a checkable fact. It either happens, on the record, or it doesn't.

00:16:50.393 --> 00:17:05.817
<v Casey>I'll put a pin in that. If it happens, the tool changed something real about who counts as an authority. If three years pass and the data's still filed as a secondary input in an appendix somewhere, the eighty-seven percent figure was the more honest prediction all along.

00:17:06.090 --> 00:17:07.482
<v Riley>Good test, either way it lands.

00:17:08.061 --> 00:17:24.621
<v Casey>There's a smaller, cheaper version of this same question that doesn't need a sequencer or three years of grant funding. Any Cariboo nation running its own creel surveys or salmon counts on a local river could start logging that data now, in a format built for their own use, whether or not DFO ever asks for it.

00:17:25.232 --> 00:17:43.440
<v Riley>Build the record before anyone outside asks to see it, so it exists on your terms whenever the question of who gets consulted comes up. If you know of a Cariboo nation already doing something like that — on salmon, on fire, on anything — write in and tell us. Feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:17:43.980 --> 00:17:51.140
<v Casey>We'd like to track this one properly instead of dropping it today and forgetting it. Enjoy the National Day for Truth and Reconciliation.

00:17:51.427 --> 00:17:53.451
<v Riley>Take care of each other out there. Back tomorrow.

00:17:53.975 --> 00:18:07.351
<v Casey>Look, I'll be straight with you — we didn't actually get a chance to talk to First Nations communications folks for this one, and that's something we should have done. If anyone's listening and wants to set that conversation up, we're all ears.